  "response": "정리해서 오지 않아도 괜찮습니다. 오히려 그 '정리가 안 되는 상태' 자체가 지금 겪고 있는 걸 잘 보여주는 것 같기도 하고요.\n\n말씀하신 걸 들으면, 머리로 이해한 것과 몸이 한 반응 사이에 간극이 있는 것 같아요. 평소엔 웃고 넘길 일이었는데, 그날은 말하기 전에 이미 돌아누워버렸고, 나중에야 '내가 왜 그랬지' 하고 되돌아보는 거죠. 그리고 위궤양이나 이를 가는 것도, 아마 본인이 자각하기 전에 이미 몸이 뭔가 밀어내고 있었던 건 아닐까 싶기도 하고요.\n\n그 다툼이 있기 전, 며칠이나 몇 주 동안 어땠는지 기억나는 게 있을까요? 거창한 일이 아니어도 괜찮습니다.",
  "reasoning": "The client is sharing several things:\n1. They hesitated to come to counseling and feel unprepared\n2. They had a minor argument with their girlfriend where they reacted coldly (turned away without speaking)\n3. They couldn't understand why they reacted that way - felt like their body responded first\n4. They've been diagnosed with a stomach ulcer\n5. They grind their teeth at night (jaw tightness)\n6. They're wondering if all these things are connected or if they're just being overly sensitive\n\nWhat they need right now:\n- Acknowledgment that it's okay to come without a clear plan\n- Validation that their experience is real and worth exploring\n- Not rushing to conclusions or diagnosing\n- Gently opening up the thread about the body's response, which seems to be the core confusion\n- Not overwhelming them with too many questions or interpretations\n\nI should acknowledge their hesitation, validate that coming without a clear plan is fine, and gently reflect back what I'm hearing - the disconnect between what they expected to happen (laughing it off) and what actually happened (cold silence, body responding first). I can also gently acknowledge the physical symptoms without jumping to conclusions about causation, and perhaps ask about what was going on before the argument, since the body responding \"first\" suggests accumulated tension.\n\nI want to avoid:\n- Mechanically repeating their words\n- Jumping to \"these are all connected, it's stress\"\n- Asking too many questions\n- Being too clinical or detached\n\nLet me write something that feels warm, present, and gently curious about the body's response without being directive.\n",
